Formatting commentary with Claude...

  (commentary generated by claude-fable-5)
Risk tone rebuilt overnight after the US Treasury unexpectedly said it will more than double buybacks of long-dated debt — a clear signal Washington wants borrowing costs lower after long yields hit multi-decade highs. The long end led the relief: Ultra Bond futures jumped 1.3% and the Long Bond 1.1%, while the front end barely moved, flattening the recent steepener. Equity-index futures firmed (S&P +0.35%, Russell +0.8%), Asia ripped — the Nikkei +1.9% and KOSPI +4.6%, the latter helped by a massive semiconductor buyback calming the AI-capex jitters — and the dollar sold off across the board (EUR +0.7%, JPY +0.7%, KRW +2%).

The fiscal backdrop hasn't changed, though: heavy issuance and inflation uncertainty keep term premium structurally elevated, so treat this as intervention-driven respite, not a regime shift. The weaker dollar and the whiff of debt monetization lit up precious metals: gold surged 2.2% to 4,517, silver +1.8%, platinum +2.8% and palladium +2.4% — the complex trading as the cleanest hedge on fiscal dominance and sticky inflation, while copper lagged on soft China signals. Energy is feeding the same inflation story: diesel is the pressure point, with heating oil and gasoil cracks at fresh record highs, Brent (+0.35% at 91.34) testing its 100-DMA, and Iran-Hormuz tensions — the UAE cutting economic ties with Tehran — keeping a geopolitical bid under crude.

Henry Hub jumped 3.5%. Elsewhere, Trump delayed 50% Canada tariffs three days on a tentative deal, easing one trade overhang; row-crop futures firmed with soybeans +1.1% and cotton +1.8% on the softer dollar.

